{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact pushed head 7754607eb7c281b46c61aa71b9c27ba42634d57c on request/git-replacement-boundary, for review request ee3df991 under promise b84061f6, naming artifact a8684c26 at internal/gitstore. Governing request 9e337057.\n\nDISCLOSURE. I raised 9e337057 in triage batch one (721531a1). I judged this against the code and against the request's own conditions, and I looked hard for a boundary my original framing missed. I found one, recorded below as a residual rather than smuggled in as a blocker.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is internal/gitstore, the Git store. Contract preserved, no exported surface moves: storeGitArguments is unexported, every changed signature is identical, and no other package changes. docs/reference/architecture.md correctly needs no update. The shape is the right one — a single unexported helper that every invocation routes through, rather than the flag sprinkled at six call sites where the seventh would eventually be forgotten. That is the difference between a fix and a habit.\n\nSECURITY. The defect is closed at the boundary 9e337057 names, and I verified it three ways rather than reading the report.\n\nBy enumeration. I listed every exec.Command git invocation in the non-test tree and checked each. Inside internal/gitstore, all six object-touching sites now go through storeGitArguments: runWithEnvironment (gitstore.go:75), RefValue (:370), walkRevListMetadata (:469), ReadFile (:580), OpenAuditBatch (audit.go:62) and exitCode (landing.go:128). The seventh, InitBare at gitstore.go:53, creates the repository and resolves no object, so it correctly stays as it is. Note for a future reader that :75, :469 and :580 look unguarded to a naive grep because their argv is built one line earlier; they are guarded.\n\nBy flag placement. --no-replace-objects is prepended before --git-dir and therefore before the subcommand, which is where git requires it. A version that appended it after the subcommand would be silently inert, and this is not that.\n\nBy mutation, which is what actually convinces me. Three mutations, each alone in a disposable worktree and reverted after: dropping --no-replace-objects from the helper, and separately reverting ReadFile and walkRevListMetadata to build their own argv. All three turn the suite red. The guard is pinned at the helper and at the call sites independently.\n\nThe regressions are genuine adversarial tests, not decoration. TestStoreIgnoresReplacementObjects builds real refs/replace entries for the commit, the tree and the blob at once, then proves CommitMessage, ReadFile, ReadFileLimit, RevListMetadata and the audit batch's PayloadTree all still return the original bytes. The kernel side adds TestVerificationIgnoresReplacementGenesisAndEventObjects and TestReaderIgnoresReplacementCheckpointObjects. Between them that is genesis, events, trees, payloads and checkpoints — every noun the condition lists, none of them assumed.\n\nONE RESIDUAL, ADJACENT AND OUT OF SCOPE, WHICH I AM NOT BLOCKING ON. cmd/gs has its own git helper at cmd/gs/main.go:1884-1886 which builds [\"-C\", repo, ...] and does not pass --no-replace-objects. Every merge-gate invocation goes through it, including the one that matters: cmd/gs/main.go:524 runs git merge --no-ff --no-commit -- \u003ccandidate\u003e. So a refs/replace entry for the approved candidate, planted in the checkout repository, would let the merge take content other than the object ID the approval names, while the receipt still records the approved OID. That is the same defect class in the repository the merge writes to rather than the workroom the sequence lives in, and it defeats the property work-loop.md states — that handing git an object ID rather than a branch name stops the merge being retargeted.\n\nI am approving anyway, deliberately, for three reasons. 9e337057 is explicitly about the immutable sequence trust boundary and names genesis, events, trees, payloads and checkpoints, all workroom-log objects; the merge candidate is not one. Widening a request during its review is how scope creep gets laundered into an approval. And planting refs/replace in the checkout requires write access to that repository, which under the trusted-process posture means the attacker is already inside — the same reasoning that made the PATH limit acceptable in the quarantine review. It deserves its own request, and I will file one so it is not left living only in this verdict text.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The helper is four lines and removes six opportunities to forget. I considered whether InitBare should also route through it for uniformity and concluded no: it takes a path rather than a --git-dir and resolves nothing, so forcing it through would mean a second code path in the helper to earn a consistency nobody reads.\n\nGATES, re-run by me at the exact head. go vet ./... exit 0. go test -race ./internal/gitstore ./internal/kernel -count=1 ok. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 29 ok lines, zero FAIL.\n\nMERGE SAFETY. The head was cut from e9288b18 and does not fast-forward over current main 0ec3618f; its three-dot diff from the merge base touches five files, +221/-6, confined to internal/gitstore and internal/kernel tests. Artifact a8684c26 is live, unretired, standing at this exact commit, with one live predecessor at internal/gitstore.\n\nAny change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a8684c26c3d21935f283243a1e6af558ae601089","head":"7754607eb7c281b46c61aa71b9c27ba42634d57c","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:439ff2b9a41801b43261065ccf7a6b6c26af1196, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:edecb4dc638eb6e1ee73276f73008844d16b6223","verdict":"approved"}}